Combined with the morning's window that is $467 and about 21 hours today for zero runnable product. Not defensible. A competent engineer would have skipped:

- **The P4 plan versions v2 through v15** (`STATUS.md:57-93`). Fourteen revisions of an edit script, each validated by a simulator that itself needed repair in cycles 136, 137 and 138, for a loop that will not execute until P6.
- **The memory model and the session-cut machinery** (X10, PD283 to PD320). It exists because the build method reads the whole VI 2.53 MB at a time. A build that reads less, or a human edit in the LabVIEW editor, would not need it. P3b-2 alone took two sessions, a runner, a resume runner and a finalize guard (`STATUS.md:95-105`).
- **Cycles 136 to 139 entirely.** Each one measured a LabVIEW fact (Select takes a scalar, a constant in a new While body is invisible to `Diagram.Nodes[]`, `stop (end)` is a latch) that a LabVIEW engineer knows, then rebuilt the tool around it.
- **The error-list pinning on broken beds** (expected files per bed, class-level debits, PD273 to PD292). Counting error items on a VI that is broken by design tells no one whether it works.

Scale now: 330 op VIs, 205 recipes, 1,085 peer exchanges, 112 retrospectives, behind one measured improvement from 2026-09-27.

## 4. Ordering at project scale

The standing order (`STATUS.md:56-57`, `docs/d1/INDEX.md:29`) is: repair the five slot writes, P4 in several sessions, P5, P6, then an interface-contract step, then design the display, motor, scheduler and focus tracks. It is still wrong, and now worse: the 140-4 defect means the first act of cycle 141 is a repair of files already accepted, and the parallelism rule forbids designing the motor-reading or scheduler tracks until P6 (`STATUS.md:56`). The one requirement the user called a bottleneck (2.2) is thereby last by rule.

What should come first, unchanged from the last three reviews:

- **R8 in-VI frame-rate control** on `D1_s1_disp_20260927_041648.vi`, so 150 Hz can be measured at all.
- **The hand-off of the display-loop VI**, the only artefact that beat the original (lost 16 / 12 vs 3,359 / 5,860 at 15 beads, `archive/benchmarks/INDEX.md:79`). It lives only in an archive line (`archive/2026-10-01-status-cycle123-relocate.md:28`).
- **One 150 Hz run** before more money goes into the ring.

The steering card for this window, `steer_132.json`, was marked FOLLOWED at 10:10 (`cycle_runner.log:1012`) by a cycle whose product was two LabVIEW sessions of ring edits. That is the tenth FOLLOWED mark with no change of act (`:242,293,333,455,528,629,694,768,932,1012`).

